 { In our final section of the chapter, we'll cover some of the most common methods or techniques for protecting the centre. These are: 1. Anticipating pawn breaks 2. Changing the structure 3. Tactics & calculation 4. Color complex strategy 5. Transforming the advantage Let's now turn to the first of these. Here, White has the better central control with the d4/e5 chain. However, Black is threatening to undermine this chain by playing the ...c5 pawn break, so White must be alert. } 1. { -KEY- } Ne2 $1 { This move would have been the most positional approach, preparing to meet Black's potential pawn break on c5. } ( 1. Bh6 $2 { In the game White played this move, giving up all of his advantage. } Bxh6 $1 2. Qxh6 { The queen by herself does not pose a serious threat to the Black king, while now White's pawn on d4 has lost its support. } c5 $1 3. Qd2 $2 { Immediately going wrong. } ( 3. Ne2 { was best, though after } Qb6 $13 { Black has a lot of pressure. } ) 
cxd4 4. Qxd4 Rc8 5. O-O Rc4 6. Qd2 Qb6 $19 { White's position is falling apart, with the b2 pawn about to be captured. } ) 
 ( 1. O-O $5 { would also have been a good way of continuing. } c5 2. Ne2 $1 { White may not yet have played c2-c3, but they can do so on the next move since Black is not yet ready to capture on d4. } cxd4 $6 { The problem with this move is that now the e6 pawn is too weak: } 3. Nxd4 Kf7 { Black has to awkwardly defend the pawn. } 4. f4 $1 { White uses the f-pawn as a way of keeping the central structure solid, keeping a big advantage. This approach could be characterised as a hybrid approach, interweaving dynamic ideas with positional play. } ) 
c5 2. c3 $1 cxd4 3. cxd4 { The structure is solid, and White now has a game-winning edge due to the enforced passivity of both of Black's minor pieces. The only things they must do is to complete their development and make sure that Black is unable to stir up significant counterplay on the queenside. Let's see how play might unfold. } Qb6 4. Kf2 { The king is best placed on f2, as White will want to play h2-h4-h5 soon to soften up Black's kingside. } Rfc8 5. h4 Rc4 { For now Black appears to be making progress on the queenside, but White has identified a good piece configuration that will keep things under control. } 6. h5 Rac8 7. Qd3 $1 { This is White's idea. The attack against g6 robs Black of the time to take on b2. } gxh5 8. Rxh5 Nf8 9. b3 $1 { White's queenside setup is very harmonious, robbing Black of any chance to make further progress. } Rc2 10. Rc1 $1 { Challenging the only active piece. } Rxc1 11. Bxc1 $18 { Black controlling the open c-file is not meaningful, as White has all the possible entry points covered. Over the coming moves, White will continue to build up the pressure on the kingside The key behind White's success was to anticipate the pawn break available to Black and prepare to meet it with c2-c3. While in this example it was possible to maintain the ideal pawn chain, this will not always be the case. Sometimes the best course of action will be to give up part of your central chain, as in our next example. } 
