 { In some situations, you may have to make a change to the structure in order to be able to maintain central control. } 1. { -KEY- } dxc5 $1 { The only path to an advantage! By changing the structure White prevents the d4 pawn from becoming a source of counterplay for the opponent. } ( 1. Qe2 $6 { White cannot hope to keep the structure as is, since Black's pieces are well-placed to pressure the d4 point. } cxd4 $1 2. cxd4 Nc6 { Suddenly the d4 pawn is feeling a little shaky. } 3. Nc3 ( { If } 3. Be3 { then after } Nd5 { the bishop would come under fire, so White develops the knight instead. } ) 
Bg4 $1 { Threatening to capture on d4 next. } 4. Be3 dxe5 $1 { By exchanging a second pair of pawns, the black queen is ready to influence the game along the d-file. } 5. dxe5 Bxf3 { Stripping White of the last defender of the d4 square. } 6. Rxf3 Nd4 $1 7. Bxd4 { White is forced to part ways with the bishop pair. } Qxd4 8. Kh1 Rfd8 $11 { Black has no problems here in spite of White's f4/e5 pawn chain, since they have reduced the material on the board and activated their pieces. You might notice that this way of playing is similar to the examples seen in the 'Overextension' section of the chapter on Space. } ) 
dxc5 2. Qe2 Qc7 3. Nbd2 Bg4 { Black's bishop does not have many squares to choose from, so they decide to give it up in the hope that an exchange of minor pieces will ease the space constraints. } ( 3... f6 $2 { This pawn break is not a good idea for Black here, since after } 4. exf6 exf6 { If ...Bxf6 instead the Black structure is left fractured and permanently weak. } 5. f5 $18 { White cuts off ...f5 ideas for Black while also launching a nasty attack on the kingside. } ) 
4. h3 Bxf3 5. Qxf3 { White now adds the bishop pair to their list of positional assets. } Nc6 6. Ne4 { Targeting the c5 pawn. } Nd7 7. Be3 Rad8 8. Rad1 { White's pieces are well-placed, while Black is struggling to find any targets. } ( { White should avoid falling into the trap after } 8. Nxc5 $2 Nxc5 9. Bxc5 Nxe5 $19 { when suddenly Black is the side that is winning. } ) 
b6 $2 { Now Black's knight on d7 is starved of forward jumps, restricting Black's activity. } 9. h4 $18 { With everything under control in the centre, White is now ready to shift focus to the kingside. Black's complete lack of counterplay translates into an objectively lost position. The key idea behind White's strategy was changing the structure with 1.dxc5, after which there were no convincing pawn breaks or ways of generating piece activity for Black. Once the situation in the centre had been consolidated, White then turned to an attack on the kingside. } 
