 { Positional understanding by itself is usually not sufficient, you must also be ready to take advantage of tactical opportunities as well as commit to calculating lines that at times can be quite challenging to work out. This is the topic we'll explore in this example and the next. This position was reached in an online game between Magnus Carlsen and GM Minh Le. White has the superior central control with the pawn on d4, but the weak square on f4 is a source of counterplay for Black. Can you find the strong 2-move sequence that Magnus found to not only maintain, but even increase his central advantage? } 1. { -KEY- } Nb5 $1 { A very clever idea, indirectly addressing the weakness of the f4 square. } ( 1. Ne4 $2 { This move seems more natural, but in fact after } Qf4 $11 { Black has no problems due to the exchange of queens and the weakness of the f4 square. } ) 
Qd8 ( { The point is that if } 1... Qf4 $6 { then after } 2. Qxf4 Nxf4 3. Nxc7 $16 { White wins a pawn. } ) 
2. c4 $1 { Correct! White now turns his attention to kicking the knight away. } Nf6 3. Rg1 $16 { White already has a near-winning position, as now the advanced g-pawn is an asset in the context of a looming kingside attack while the weak square on f4 is inaccessible to the Black pieces. In your games, tactical resources like the above may help to bail you out in situations where your central advantage is delicate or under threat in any way. Let's take a look at one more example of this. } 
