 { Black's opening play has gone horribly wrong and White's powerful central pawns leave them with an objectively winning advantage. However, White will have to demonstrate precise calculation if they want to maximise their advantage. Can you find the best move? } 1. { -KEY- } Bb5+ $1 { Correct! This move is best, with a forcing line in mind. } ( 1. Qxd4 $2 { The exchange of queens would throw away the advantage. } cxd4 2. Nb5 Nxd5 3. Nxd4 Bc5 $17 { Black is left with the healthier structure and better development. } ) 
Bd7 { Black is in trouble, but the piece pressure offers practical chances for the opponent to go wrong. } 2. Qe2 $1 { White keeps the queens on the board. This is ideal given that White's central pawns offer fantastic attacking opportunities. However, this move requires White to have calculated the consequences of Black's next capture. } ( 2. Bf4 { would also be a path to an advantage, but the text move is strongest. } ) 
Nxd5 { Objectively this move is a mistake, but it is the most testing move in practice as White has only one path to an advantage. } ( 2... Be7 $2 { A quiet move does not trouble White. e.g. } 3. Nf3 Qg4 4. Bf4 O-O 5. Bd3 $18 { White's central advantage is safe. } ) 
3. e6 $1 { The key idea! Black's king is in trouble. } fxe6 ( 3... Bxb5 { This move will also not save Black. } 4. Qxb5 Kd8 5. Qxb7 { There are many winning continuations, but the most direct works well. } Nxc3 6. Bg5 f6 7. bxc3 Qxc3 8. Kf2 Qxa1 9. Ne2 $1 { The last precise move that was needed. Now Black has to give up their queen since a move like ...Qe5 leaves Black in trouble after Rd1+. } Qxh1 10. Qxh1 fxg5 11. Qxa8 $18 { White's queen is far stronger than Black's rook and bishop. We can see from this analysis that sometimes the variations that will test your calculation skill are those that never actually appear on the board! } ) 
4. Qxe6+ Kd8 ( 4... Be7 { loses a piece after } 5. Bxd7 Nxd7 6. Nxd5 $18 ) 
5. Bg5+ $1 { Again, White must find this move in order to keep a winning advantage. } Be7 6. Qxd5 Qxd5 7. Bxe7+ $1 { This is the point. Now Black's king will be exposed on e7. } Kxe7 8. Nxd5+ { By capturing the queen with check, Black is not in time to recover their material by playing ...Bxb5. The crazy thing about this entire line is that White needed to have seen up to this point six moves earlier when playing 2.Qe2, highlighting the key role that calculation plays in defending a positional advantage. Next up, we'll revisit the topic of color complexes in the context of protecting the centre. } 
