 { In some instances, you will find yourself with a more complex power struggle in the centre. In the following position, White stands better because of the occupation of the weak square on e5. However, things could quickly sour if they allow Black to mobilise their central pawns. } 1. { -KEY- } Re1 $1 { Defending the knight and stopping Black from mobilising their pawns. } ( 1. Nf3 $2 { In the game White responded to the attack on their knight by retreating, but this move is a major mistake. } e5 $1 { Mobilising the centre, and threatening ...e4 next. Black is already practically winning. } ) 
Qc7 2. Bf4 $1 { White sets up a bind along the dark squares. } g5 { Targeting the defender of the knight. } 3. Bxf5 $1 { A powerful in-between move. By removing the knight, Black no longer controls the g3 square. } Bxf5 4. Bg3 $18 { White has firm control over the dark squares, and soon they will be ready to exploit their 3 vs 2 pawn majority on the queenside. In essence, White pursued a color complex strategy in order to keep Black's pawns from advancing. I find this example particularly instructive in that it shows the two sides of the battle for central dominance. Black had the superior pawn presence, while White had the superior piece control. In the end, White was able to keep the pawns at bay and so was able to keep the advantage. Our final example involves willingly giving up central pawn control and is an important option to keep in mind in order to maintain flexible thinking at all times. } 
