 { In this example White has the greater central pawn presence, but on the other hand Black has a lot of piece pressure against it. Can you find the only winning move for White? } 1. { -KEY- } O-O $1 { White remains tactically alert, and offers up the centre. Having avoided the tactical pitfalls, White is now in fact winning since they have a concrete idea in store after Black captures on d4. } ( 1. d5 $2 { In the game White chose to advance, but this walks into a well-disguised tactical blow: } Nxe4 $1 { A beautiful move, converting Black's piece activity into material gains and destroying the centre in the process. } 2. Nxe4 ( 2. Qxe4 Qxf2 3. Kd1 Rad8 $19 { White is lost as their king is trapped in the centre of the board and, to make matters worse, the knight on g3 is hanging. } ) 
 ( 2. dxe6 Bxf2 $19 ) 
Bxd5 $19 { There is no way to defend the knight on e4, so Black will recover the piece on their next move and emerge with a winning position. } ) 
 ( 1. dxe5 $2 { is also a mistake, and is once again met by a powerful piece sacrifice: } Bc4 $1 { Cutting off the exit route for White's king. } 2. exf6 Rad8 ( { Threatening } 3. O-O Rd1 { when after } 4. Qxd1 Qxf2 { is mate. } ) 
3. Bf4 Bb6 $1 { Threatening ...Ba5+. } 4. b4 { Stopping the threat, but leaving a weak square on c3 that Black immediately exploits. } Bxf2 $1 { The finishing blow. } 5. Qxf2 Qc3 6. Bd2 { The only alternative to avoid getting mated is to play Qd2 instead, but there White loses a tremendous amount of material. } Qxa1 7. Bc1 Qxc1 ) 
Bxd4 ( { If } 1... Kh7 { then White could continue with d4-d5 and keep their centre. However, it turns out that they would have an even stronger move. } 2. Bxh6 $1 Kxh6 3. dxe5 $18 { White recovers their piece with a winning position, since if the Black knight moves then Qh5# would follow. } ) 
 ( 1... Bc4 { It's worth nothing that an idea like this move doesn't quite work, not only because White could play dxe5 here, but also because White has great influence on the f5 square, which belongs to what we could call the 'expanded centre'. So for example, White might continue: } 2. Nhf5 { when the best line for Black runs as follows: } Bxf1 3. Nxg7 $1 Bxd4 4. Qxf6 Qxf2 5. Qxf2 Bxf2 6. Kxf2 Kxg7 7. Nf5 Kg6 8. Kxf1 $18 { The two minor pieces are much stronger than Black's extra rook, so White is winning. } ) 
2. Bxh6 $1 { Correct! White relies on this tactical possibility to ensure a winning advantage in spite of losing their edge in the centre. } Bxb2 3. Rae1 $1 { The entire queenside is abandoned, but the kingside attack is already unstoppable. } Kh8 4. Nh5 $1 Nxh5 5. Qxh5 gxh6 6. Qxh6+ Kg8 7. Re3 { Black will soon be checkmated. In this example we once again saw the power of tactical alertness, both defensively and offensively. And, while the focus of this section has been on protecting your centre, it's important to consider that on occasion the best course of action will be to focus on more dynamic paths to an advantage instead of fixating on maintaining your pawn formation. This brings the section, and indeed the chapter, to a close. I hope you've enjoyed it and that it's helped you to build upon the work done in previous chapters. Next, you can review the chapter summary, or jump straight into the puzzle set if you'd like. } 
